    <code code="51848-0" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="Evaluation note"/>
    <title>Assessment</title>
    <text><list><item>Paroxysmal atrial fibrillation, first detected four days before intake while running, triggered by hyperthyroidism; CHA2DS2-VASc 1; synthetic modern episode linked to his documented atrial fibrillation: active, confirmed [S]</item><item>Graves&apos; disease with hyperthyroidism; synthetic modern diagnosis linked to his documented Graves&apos; disease: active, confirmed [S]</item></list></text>

  </section>
</component>

<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="2.16.840.1.113883.10.20.22.2.10" extension="2014-06-09"/>
    <code code="18776-5" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="Plan of care note"/>
    <title>Plan of Treatment</title>
    <text><list><item>Rate control with metoprolol succinate 50 mg daily; no antiarrhythmic drug or cardioversion while thyrotoxic, because most thyrotoxic atrial fibrillation reverts once thyroid levels are normal; taper the beta-blocker once he is no longer thyrotoxic. Guideline: 2023 ACC/AHA/ACCP/HRS Guideline for the Diagnosis and Management of Atrial Fibrillation (2023); 2016 American Thyroid Association Guidelines for Diagnosis and Management of Hyperthyroidism and Other Causes of Thyrotoxicosis (2016); CMID: Clinical Monitoring Intelligence Database, atrial fibrillation and hypothyroidism monographs (2026) [S]</item><item>Stroke-risk and bleeding-risk scoring (CHA2DS2-VASc 1 for age 65 to 74; HAS-BLED 1 for age over 65) with a shared decision to continue apixaban 5 mg twice daily while thyrotoxic, then reassess once sustained sinus rhythm is confirmed after he is no longer thyrotoxic. Guideline: 2023 ACC/AHA/ACCP/HRS Guideline for the Diagnosis and Management of Atrial Fibrillation (2023); CMID: Clinical Monitoring Intelligence Database, atrial fibrillation and hypothyroidism monographs (2026) [S]</item><item>Methimazole 20 mg daily after a baseline blood count and liver tests, with written advice to stop it and call at once for fever, sore throat, mouth ulcers, jaundice, or dark urine; stop three days before radioactive iodine. Guideline: 2016 American Thyroid Association Guidelines for Diagnosis and Management of Hyperthyroidism and Other Causes of Thyrotoxicosis (2016) [S]</item><item>Endocrinology consultation to confirm Graves&apos; disease, assess the eyes, and choose definitive treatment (radioactive iodine, long-term methimazole, or surgery). Guideline: 2016 American Thyroid Association Guidelines for Diagnosis and Management of Hyperthyroidism and Other Causes of Thyrotoxicosis (2016) [S]</item><item>After radioactive iodine: free T4 and total T3 within one to two months and then every four to six weeks until stable; levothyroxine once hypothyroid, started below full replacement; TSH four to eight weeks after each dose change, at six months once stable, then yearly; target TSH 0.4 to 4.0 mIU/L. Guideline: 2016 American Thyroid Association Guidelines for Diagnosis and Management of Hyperthyroidism and Other Causes of Thyrotoxicosis (2016); Guidelines for the Treatment of Hypothyroidism: Prepared by the American Thyroid Association Task Force on Thyroid Hormone Replacement (2014); CMID: Clinical Monitoring Intelligence Database, atrial fibrillation and hypothyroidism monographs (2026) [S]</item><item>Atrial fibrillation monitoring at every visit (heart rate and rhythm, blood pressure, weight, symptoms, activity, alcohol, tobacco), ECG when indicated, echocardiogram at diagnosis, CHA2DS2-VASc and HAS-BLED yearly, creatinine and blood count at baseline and yearly on apixaban, and sleep apnea screening at diagnosis and yearly. Guideline: 2023 ACC/AHA/ACCP/HRS Guideline for the Diagnosis and Management of Atrial Fibrillation (2023); CMID: Clinical Monitoring Intelligence Database, atrial fibrillation and hypothyroidism monographs (2026) [S]</item><item>Return to running in steps once the heart rate is controlled, aiming for about 210 minutes a week of moderate to vigorous activity; limit alcohol to no more than one drink a day. Guideline: 2023 ACC/AHA/ACCP/HRS Guideline for the Diagnosis and Management of Atrial Fibrillation (2023); CMID: Clinical Monitoring Intelligence Database, atrial fibrillation and hypothyroidism monographs (2026) [S]</item></list></text>

  </section>
